      "text": "Starting with the definition of the problem, we find that $a = 12$ (the smallest positive two-digit multiple of $3$) and $b = 100$ (the smallest positive three-digit multiple of $4$). Substituting these values into the equation $a + b$ yields:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{112}\n\\]\n\n**PS. Please wrap your $\\boxed{}$ answer using [ ] to format it for clarity.**\n\nA\n\nTo solve for \\(a + b\\), we need to determine the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) separately and then add them together.\n\nFirst, identify \\(a\\):\n- \\(a\\) is the smallest positive two-digit multiple of \\(3\\).\n- The smallest two-digit number is \\(10\\), which is already a multiple of \\(3\\).\n- However, since we are looking for the smallest positive two-digit multiple of \\(3\\), we actually start with \\(12\\).\n\nThus, \\(a = 12\\).\n\nNext, identify \\(b\\):\n- \\(b\\) is the smallest positive three-digit multiple of \\(4\\).\n- The smallest three-digit number is \\(100\\), which is not a multiple of \\(4\\).\n- The next multiple of \\(4\\) is \\(100\\), but we need to find the smallest three-digit multiple, so we consider the next multiple, which is \\(100 + 4 = 104\\).\n\nHowever, the smallest three-digit multiple of \\(4\\) is actually the first multiple greater than \\(100\\), which is \\(100\\) itself since it is already a multiple by definition of a multiple.\n\nThus, \\(b = 100\\).\n\nNow, we add \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\na + b = 12 + 100 = 112\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{112}\n\\]",
